So here are the main principles of the circular economy:

{{Another Circular Economy Starting Place}}

They are important aspects of [[Regenerative Design]] — of [[giving back at every stage of an objects life|How might we design a chair that gives back at every stage of its life cycle?]]

In the context of what these principles mean, here's how the [[Oka Chair]] does them… and here is how you can try to think about them for your own projects

1. [[Eliminate waste and pollution]]

The Oka chair attacks wastefulness in multiple ways. If you look at the supply chain of a normal mass produced chair, well, it might span the entire globe, multiple times. For starters the oka chair drastically shrinks the supply chain. Everything in the design of the Oka Chair comes from the greater Maryland materail shed/bio region. This means that transports resources are minimized. It not only uses local materials, but also local manuifacturing. Again, very little resources for transport. 

Then, the materials themselves. The supplier of the wool (NAME?), she was complaining about waste wool, all the bits and pieces that are too small to spin to yarn, or too hard to use in other ways. This "waste wool" [[Common Objects]] decided to use as the stuffing in their cushions. This solves one problem for common objects — where to source a replacement for foam, and solves a serious issue for the wool farmer — what to do with the waste wool. Well, using it as cushion stuffer is working wonders so far.

This same set of ideas carries through all the material and manufacturing choices — [[design for disassembly]], organic hem from a different farm. etc.

2. [[Circulate products and materials]]

All natural materials, all can be composted…

Chair is easily disassemblable

What else?

3. [[Regenerate nature]]

By doing these things, the play yard is really a good place for things like birthdays, I'll see about reducing the gym numbers outside.

